WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:04.629
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.629 --> 00:00:10.630
How cool would it be to be able to place a
character representing us in a digital film

00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:11.960
or a computer game?

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:17.939
Of course, it would clearly be an extremely
laborious task to digitize the 3D geometry

00:00:17.939 --> 00:00:19.779
and the albedo map of our face.

00:00:19.779 --> 00:00:26.679
This albedo map means a texture, a colored
pattern that describes how our skin reflects

00:00:26.679 --> 00:00:28.349
and absorbs light.

00:00:28.349 --> 00:00:33.550
Capturing such a representation is clearly
a very lengthy and expensive process.

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:39.450
So get this completely crazy idea: this technique
creates this full digital representation of

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:43.460
any face from no more than one simple photograph.

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:48.880
We can even get historical figures in our
digital universe, all we need is one photograph

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:49.880
of them.

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:53.990
And now, feast your eyes on these incredible
results.

00:00:53.990 --> 00:00:59.470
After taking a photograph, this technique
creates two of these albedo maps: one is a

00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:06.360
complete, low frequency map, which records
the entirety of the face, but only contains

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:07.360
the rough details.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:11.140
The other albedo map contains finer details,
but in return, is incomplete.

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:16.600
Do you remember the texture synthesis methods
that we discussed earlier in the series?

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:21.630
The input was a tiny patch of image with a
repetitive structure, and after learning the

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:27.420
statistical properties of these structures,
it was possible to continue them indefinitely.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:33.180
The key insight is that we can also do something
akin to that here as well - we take this incomplete

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:37.220
albedo map, and try to synthesize the missing
details.

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:39.479
Pretty amazing idea indeed!

00:01:39.479 --> 00:01:44.480
The authors of the paper invoke a classical
learning algorithm, a convolutional neural

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:46.140
network to accomplish that.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:50.710
The deeper the neural network we use, the
more high-frequency details appear on the

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:54.350
outputs, or in other words, the crisper the
image we get.

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:59.240
In the paper, you will find a detailed description
of their crowdsourced user study that was

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:03.850
used to validate this technique, including
the user interface and the questions being

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:04.850
asked.

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:09.750
There are also some comparisons against PatchMatch,
one of the landmark techniques for texture

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:13.450
synthesis that we have also talked about in
an earlier episode.

00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:18.440
It's pretty amazing to see this Two Minute
Papers knowledge base grow and get more and

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:19.440
more intertwined.

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:22.371
I hope you're enjoying the process as much
as I do!

